1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:11,870
Welcome to Opengrader. For the smoothest run, a class folder holds four files: the scanned submissions, the QA answer key, the class roster, and the blank template.

2
00:00:12,470 --> 00:00:24,850
Only the submissions and the QA file are required. The roster puts a name and student ID on every paper, and the template shows Opengrader where each answer is, so the run is faster and more accurate.

3
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:37,380
And if you don't have a QA file yet, Opengrader can create one for you, from an answer key you already have, in Word, PDF, text, or Markdown, or with help from your own AI assistant.

4
00:00:38,330 --> 00:00:43,870
Here, all four files are in the folder, so we run opengrader run on the class folder.

5
00:00:44,280 --> 00:00:55,580
Opengrader finds the scanned bundle, the answer key, the roster, and the template, then starts the scan check, looking at every page for rotation, order, blank pages, and scan quality.

6
00:00:58,480 --> 00:01:09,160
Next, the split stage matches the scanned pages to the blank template, and cuts the bundle into one PDF per student. Here, twelve pages become six submissions.

7
00:01:11,267 --> 00:01:17,797
The extract stage reads all six submissions in parallel, and the roster matches each paper to a student.

8
00:01:18,267 --> 00:01:24,647
Then auto-grade checks the answers against the QA file, and a summary lists every stage that ran.

9
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:35,460
When the pipeline finishes, the grading interface opens, with the questions and students on the left, and the question's details on the right.

10
00:01:38,147 --> 00:01:46,207
We open question one and grade it student by student. The scanned answer is shown beside the expected answer and the rubric.

11
00:01:48,187 --> 00:01:55,437
Picking a rubric item sets the score. This student circled Queue, which is correct, so they get full credit.

12
00:01:58,187 --> 00:02:04,237
Students who chose Stack or Heap are marked incorrect, and the progress counter updates as we go.

13
00:02:07,737 --> 00:02:13,477
The layout can be switched, so the scan sits below the grading panel with more room to read it.

14
00:02:15,737 --> 00:02:24,357
Comments can be added to any answer. Here we leave, Good job, on a correct answer, and, Wrong answer, on an incorrect one.

15
00:02:27,857 --> 00:02:36,307
Back on the question list, the statistics and score distribution for question one are updated, and the grading log records every grade and comment.

16
00:02:38,327 --> 00:02:44,857
Now let's return the graded work. Pressing the B key opens the Export return bundle window.

17
00:02:45,207 --> 00:02:54,447
We name the bundle folder, choose Student PDFs only, and press Export. Opengrader writes an annotated PDF for all six students.

18
00:02:55,827 --> 00:03:01,587
The new folder appears inside the class folder, with one annotated PDF per student.

19
00:03:05,087 --> 00:03:12,817
Opening a student's PDF shows the total score at the top, and each comment, like Good job, attached right next to the answer.

20
00:03:14,907 --> 00:03:23,597
Grading can also be done in the browser. Back in the terminal, we run opengrader web, with the workspace option pointing at the class folder.

21
00:03:26,033 --> 00:03:32,413
The browser opens on the assignments page, showing the quiz, its submissions, and what is left to grade.

22
00:03:32,763 --> 00:03:37,823
The answer key page lists every question, with its correct answer and rubric.

23
00:03:38,173 --> 00:03:44,153
On the grade page, the grades from the terminal are already here, including the Good job comment.

24
00:03:44,503 --> 00:03:54,483
On question two, we click a rubric item and type feedback, like Try again. It saves automatically, and Next ungraded jumps straight to the next student.

25
00:03:57,983 --> 00:04:02,353
We work through the rest of the class the same way, one click per answer.

26
00:04:05,853 --> 00:04:13,003
Review grades shows every student's scores in one table, with downloads for a CSV or the feedback PDFs.

27
00:04:13,353 --> 00:04:19,233
And the statistics page charts the mean score for each question, so you can see how the class did.

